The entire net/http package is built around a deliberately tiny interface:

```go
type Handler interface {
	ServeHTTP(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)
}
```

That's the whole definition -- one method, no return value. This is exactly the same small-interface philosophy from the Packages and Standard Library concept, where `io.Reader`'s single `Read` method let an enormous variety of unrelated types compose with functions like `io.Copy` without any of them declaring an explicit relationship to `io.Reader`. Here, anything with a method matching that exact `ServeHTTP` signature automatically satisfies `http.Handler`, implicitly, with no `implements` keyword anywhere:

```go
type greeter struct {
	name string
}

func (g greeter) ServeHTTP(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	fmt.Fprintf(w, "Hello, %s!", g.name)
}
```

`greeter` never mentions `http.Handler` anywhere in its own definition, yet a `greeter` value can be handed to anything in net/http that expects an `http.Handler`, purely because it has the right method. `ServeHTTP` itself is responsible for the entire lifetime of handling one request: reading whatever it needs from `r *http.Request` (method, URL, headers, body), and writing a response through `w http.ResponseWriter` -- there is no separate return value for the response; everything the client receives is a side effect of what your `ServeHTTP` method does to `w` before it returns.

`http.ResponseWriter` has three methods worth knowing precisely: `Header()` returns the response's header map, which you can freely modify **before** anything else happens; `WriteHeader(statusCode int)` sends the status line and headers to the client; and `Write(data []byte)` sends body bytes, implicitly calling `WriteHeader(http.StatusOK)` first **if `WriteHeader` hasn't already been called explicitly**.

This has a real, sharp consequence: **once the status code has been sent -- either via an explicit `WriteHeader` call or implicitly via the first `Write` -- it cannot be changed.** A common, genuinely easy mistake is writing some body content first and only afterward deciding an error occurred and trying to send a different status code:

```go
func buggyHandler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	fmt.Fprintln(w, "partial output...")   // implicitly sends 200 OK right here
	if somethingWentWrong() {
		w.WriteHeader(http.StatusInternalServerError)   // TOO LATE: 200 was already sent
		return
	}
}
```

This compiles and runs with no panic -- `WriteHeader` called after the status is already locked in simply logs a message like `http: superfluous response.WriteHeader call` to the server's own logs and has **no effect** on what the client actually receives, which remains a 200 with whatever partial body was already flushed. The correct discipline is to decide the final status code **before** writing any body content at all -- buffer the response into a value first if you genuinely need to inspect it before deciding on a status, or, far more commonly, simply structure the handler so all error checks happen before any successful-path writing begins.

examples.code:
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"net/http"
)

type greeter struct {
	name string
}

func (g greeter) ServeHTTP(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	fmt.Fprintf(w, "Hello, %s!", g.name)
}

func main() {
	mux := http.NewServeMux()
	mux.Handle("/hello", greeter{name: "Gopher"})
	http.ListenAndServe(":8080", mux)
}

package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"net/http"
)

func helloHandler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	fmt.Fprintln(w, "Hello from a plain function!")
}

func main() {
	mux := http.NewServeMux()
	mux.HandleFunc("/hello", helloHandler)   // internally wraps helloHandler in http.HandlerFunc(...)
	http.ListenAndServe(":8080", mux)
}

package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"net/http"
)

func main() {
	mux := http.NewServeMux()

	mux.HandleFunc("GET /users/{id}", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		id := r.PathValue("id")
		fmt.Fprintf(w, "fetching user %s", id)
	})

	mux.HandleFunc("DELETE /users/{id}", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		id := r.PathValue("id")
		fmt.Fprintf(w, "deleting user %s", id)
	})

	http.ListenAndServe(":8080", mux)
}

