encoding/json offers two related ways to turn JSON text into a Go value: `json.Unmarshal(data []byte, v interface{})`, which needs the entire JSON payload already sitting in memory as a byte slice, and `json.NewDecoder(r io.Reader).Decode(v interface{})`, which reads directly from any `io.Reader` -- streaming through the input rather than requiring it all up front. For an HTTP handler, `r.Body` is itself an `io.ReadCloser` (satisfying `io.Reader`, exactly the small-interface composition from the Packages concept), which makes the decoder the natural fit:

```go
type CreateUserRequest struct {
	Name string `json:"name"`
	Age  int    `json:"age"`
}

func createUserHandler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	var req CreateUserRequest
	if err := json.NewDecoder(r.Body).Decode(&req); err != nil {
		http.Error(w, "invalid request body", http.StatusBadRequest)
		return
	}
	// req.Name and req.Age are now populated
}
```

Using `json.Unmarshal` here would require first calling `io.ReadAll(r.Body)` to buffer the entire request body into a `[]byte`, and only then unmarshaling that byte slice -- an extra allocation and an extra full read pass that `Decode` avoids by reading and parsing incrementally as bytes arrive. For a typical small JSON API payload this difference is negligible, but for a service that must handle large or numerous request bodies without a plan for exactly how much memory each one might buffer, decoding straight from the stream is the more defensively-written default. Note the `&req` -- `Decode` needs a pointer to the destination so it can actually populate the value the caller holds, exactly the same value-vs-pointer-receiver reasoning from the Foundations module's Structs and Methods concept: passing `req` by value would let `Decode` fill in a throwaway copy that the handler never sees.

Encoding follows the same Reader/Writer symmetry: `json.NewEncoder(w io.Writer).Encode(v interface{})` writes JSON directly to any `io.Writer`, and `http.ResponseWriter` satisfies that interface, so encoding straight to the response is the idiomatic pattern:

```go
func getUserHandler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	user := User{Name: "Amy"}
	w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "application/json")   // MUST happen before Encode's first Write
	if err := json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(user); err != nil {
		log.Printf("failed to encode response: %v", err)
	}
}
```

This is a direct application of the ResponseWriter ordering rule from the Handlers and Routing concept: `Encode` writes body bytes, which -- if no status has been set explicitly yet -- implicitly locks in `200 OK` on its first internal `Write` call. Setting `Content-Type` via `w.Header().Set(...)` **after** calling `Encode` would be too late: the headers have already been sent to the client by the time `Encode`'s first byte goes out, so the header map mutation would either have no effect or trigger the same 'superfluous' warning territory covered in the previous concept, depending on exactly when it happens relative to Encode's internal writes. The correct order is always: set headers, then write body -- never the reverse, and this is one of the most common small correctness bugs in hand-written Go HTTP handlers precisely because it compiles and often even appears to work in casual testing against tools that don't strictly validate Content-Type.

examples.code:
package main

import (
	"encoding/json"
	"net/http"
)

type CreateUserRequest struct {
	Name string `json:"name"`
	Age  int    `json:"age"`
}

func createUserHandler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	var req CreateUserRequest
	if err := json.NewDecoder(r.Body).Decode(&req); err != nil {
		http.Error(w, "invalid request body: "+err.Error(), http.StatusBadRequest)
		return
	}

	w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "application/json")
	w.WriteHeader(http.StatusCreated)
	json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(req)
}

package main

import (
	"encoding/json"
	"fmt"
)

type Profile struct {
	Name     string `json:"name"`
	Nickname string `json:"nickname,omitempty"`
	password string `json:"password"`   // unexported -- the tag has NO effect here at all
}

func main() {
	p := Profile{Name: "Amy", Nickname: "", password: "secret123"}
	data, _ := json.Marshal(p)
	fmt.Println(string(data))
	// {"name":"Amy"}
	// Nickname is omitted (it's the zero value, "", and tagged omitempty)
	// password is ALWAYS omitted -- it's unexported, so json can't see it, tag or not

